update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$Before becoming the administrative and economic centre of Douala, Bonanjo was one Sawa village among others on the Wouri estuary, the waterway that shaped the whole history of the Duala people. According to Duala oral tradition, the founding lineage descends from a common ancestor, Mbedi a Mbongo, whose son Ewale a Mbedi is said to have led a migration to the mouth of the Wouri; the very name “Duala” is said to come from “Du'Ewale”, “those of Ewale”. Another version, handed down by the Bell chieftaincy itself, places the foundation in 1792, when Prince Bélè ba Doo took the head of a unified kingdom after the death of his father Doo la Makongo (King Joss), who is said to have ruled over all the Duala from Bonanjo between 1756 and 1792. These two accounts do not match perfectly: they belong to distinct oral traditions, specific to different lineages, and no independent academic source today makes it possible to decide between them.

What is better established is what followed: over the course of the 19th century, Duala society organised itself into several rival and complementary chieftaincies settled on the estuary, among which Bell, Akwa and Deido were some of the most influential. Each drew its power from river and sea trade with European ships, an intermediary role the Duala had long held on this stretch of coast. It is this commercial power that made Bonanjo a strategic prize: in July 1884, the German explorer Gustav Nachtigal had the kings Akwa, Bell and Deido sign the treaties there that paved the way for the German protectorate of Kamerun.

The most emblematic building in Bonanjo today is the Palace of the Bell kings, built in 1905 by German builders for King Auguste Manga Ndumbe. Its outline, marked by a succession of tiered roofs, earned it the nickname “the Pagoda”, given by the French writer Louis-Ferdinand Céline, who stayed in Cameroon in 1916-1917 and mentioned the building in “Journey to the End of the Night” (1932). It was in this palace that Rudolf Douala Manga Bell, son of Auguste Manga Ndumbe, lived from his accession in 1908 until his arrest in July 1914 by the German colonial administration, which had him executed the same year for his opposition to the expropriation of Duala lands. The palace is still listed as a national monument today, even though it no longer serves as a royal residence.

In 1910, the German authorities drew up an urban plan that separated the native districts from the European districts and expropriated 280 hectares of the Joss plateau, provoking fierce local opposition, a prelude to the tensions that would culminate in the Bell affair. Bonanjo then became the seat of the German and later French colonial administrations, a role as centre of power that it keeps today as the administrative and business heart of Douala.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Bonanjo is one of the historic founding villages of the Duala on the left bank of the Wouri, along with Akwa and Deido"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The Duala lineage is said to descend from a common ancestor, Mbedi a Mbongo, through his son Ewale a Mbedi, who migrated to the Wouri estuary"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The Bell chieftaincy is said to have been founded in 1792 by Prince Bélè ba Doo, after a unified kingdom led by his father Doo la Makongo (King Joss) from Bonanjo (1756-1792)"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The two origin stories (Ewale a Mbedi's migration / the 1792 foundation by Bélè ba Doo) do not match and come from distinct traditions"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "In July 1884, Gustav Nachtigal had the kings Akwa, Bell and Deido sign land cession treaties that paved the way for the German protectorate"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Palace of the Bell kings was built in 1905 by German builders for King Auguste Manga Ndumbe"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The nickname “the Pagoda” comes from the writer Louis-Ferdinand Céline, who mentions it in “Journey to the End of the Night” (1932)"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Rudolf Douala Manga Bell lived in the palace from 1908 until his arrest in July 1914, before his execution the same year"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "In 1910, the German administration cut out 280 hectares of the Joss plateau for a European district, provoking local opposition"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Palace of the Bell kings is now listed as a national monument and serves as a restaurant"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$bonanjo-palais-des-rois-bell$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$The site that would become Ebolowa appears in historical accounts at the end of the 1890s, when American Presbyterian missionaries, including Dr Adolphus C. Good, settled in the region along the Kribi-Lolodorf axis, with the approval of the German authorities, who also saw it as an opportunity to extend their administrative hold over the hinterland. Around this twofold presence, missionary and military, a German colonial post took shape on the hill known locally as Ebolowa-si.

The very name of the town carries a local legend, widespread but impossible to authenticate with certainty: a hunter named Zanga Mba is said to have come across the rotting carcass of a great ape and exclaimed “Ebolowo'o”, meaning “here is the rotten chimpanzee”; the German soldiers present are said to have misheard the word, repeating it as “Ebolowa”, which eventually became the name of the place.

It was above all the war of 1903, sometimes extended into 1904 depending on the sources, that brought Ebolowa into the history of Cameroonian resistance. The Bulu chief Evina Minko'o, from the village of Azem, in the immediate surroundings of the town, mobilised several thousand Bulu warriors, in confederation with other groups in the region, to avenge relatives killed by the Germans and to oppose their advance. The account of the siege itself differs sharply depending on the sources: oral tradition accounts relayed by the Cameroonian press report a Bulu victory, with a month-long blockade that is said to have forced the German garrison to evacuate the town towards Kribi at the end of February 1903; a more academic work on the resistance of the southern hinterland describes, on the contrary, a confrontation lasting more than a year, with heavy losses on both sides, and an outcome that was ultimately unfavourable to the Bulu, sealed by a negotiated peace. What both versions do confirm is the scale of the Bulu mobilisation (the figures range from several thousand to more than ten thousand fighters depending on the episodes and the sources) and the reality of an organised armed resistance, long after other regions of the country had already been subdued.

After this period, the whole region was gradually “pacified” by the German administration, a process historians broadly place around 1907. Ebolowa then became a lasting colonial administrative centre, and later, under French administration, a regional capital, a status it keeps today as the capital of the South region of Cameroon, an agricultural (cocoa in particular) and administrative hub of Bulu country.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "American Presbyterian missionaries, including Adolphus C. Good, settled in the Ebolowa region at the end of the 1890s"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "A German colonial post was set up on the Ebolowa-si hill in the same period"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The popular etymology of the name “Ebolowa” (“rotten chimpanzee” misheard by German soldiers)"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Bulu chief Evina Minko'o, from Azem, led an armed resistance mobilising several thousand Bulu warriors against the Germans in the early 1900s"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The precise course and outcome of the siege of Ebolowa (a Bulu victory with a German retreat towards Kribi, versus a prolonged confrontation ending in a negotiated peace unfavourable to the Bulu)"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The exact number of fighters mobilised varies widely depending on the sources (from around 6,000 to more than 14,000 depending on the episodes)"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Historians consider the region broadly “pacified” by the German administration around 1907"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Ebolowa is today the capital of the South region of Cameroon"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$ebolowa$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$In the Grassfields of western Cameroon, the figure of the traditional chief holds a place that few Western institutions make it possible to grasp straight away. Among the Bamum, this chief bears the title of Mfon (the word “fon” more broadly designating the sovereign in the neighbouring Grassfields chieftaincies, and the title of “sultan” also being commonly used since the Islamisation of the Bamum court). His office is hereditary, handed down within the kingdom's patrilineal lineages, and his powers go far beyond the ceremonial: traditionally, he commands the army, controls customary justice, and negotiates alliances and tensions with neighbouring chieftaincies and kingdoms.

In Bamum culture, however, this power is not conceived of as purely political. According to the traditional view, the chief is invested with a prestige of a sacred nature: he embodies the power of the kingdom's founding ancestors and serves as a link between the world of the living and that of the ancestors, which makes him, as much as a head of state, a guardian of the spiritual bond that holds the community together. It is precisely this double status, political and sacred, that makes the Nguon so significant: this great biennial assembly, where the Bamum people publicly question their chief's rule and can sanction him for failings, is a reminder that even a power of a sacred nature remains, among the Bamum, an accountable power. Tradition also reports that the chief is surrounded by a college of notables consulted on the major stages of the reign, a practice well documented in the neighbouring Bamileke chieftaincies of the Grassfields, but whose precise application at the Bamum court remains less firmly established in the sources consulted.

The institution survived colonisation and still exists today within Cameroon's legal framework: since a 1977 decree, traditional chieftaincies, including that of the Bamum, have been institutions recognised by the State, ranked in three degrees according to their historical and territorial importance. The chief keeps the status of an auxiliary of the administration and a role of arbitration in customary, family, inheritance or land disputes, within the limits set by law. Today's Bamum traditional chief thus moves between two worlds: heir to a deeply rooted ancestral and ritual legitimacy, and a recognised, though regulated, actor of the contemporary Cameroonian administration.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "In the Bamum kingdom, the sovereign bears the title of Mfon, also rendered as “sultan” since the Islamisation of the court"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The office of chief is hereditary, handed down within patrilineal lineages"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The chief traditionally commands the army, controls customary justice and negotiates with neighbouring chieftaincies"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "According to the traditional Bamum view, the chief is invested with a sacred prestige and embodies the bond between the living and the ancestors"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Nguon gives concrete form to the chief's accountability: he is publicly questioned there about his rule and can be sanctioned"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The chief is traditionally surrounded by a college of notables consulted at the major stages of the reign"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Since decree no. 77/245 of 1977, Cameroonian traditional chieftaincies, including the Bamum chieftaincy, have been institutions recognised by the State, ranked in three degrees"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The traditional chief today keeps the status of an auxiliary of the administration and a role of arbitration in customary disputes, within the limits set by law"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$fon-chef-traditionnel-bamoun$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$The Ngondo is the great traditional assembly of the Sawa people, the vast group of coastal communities of Cameroon of which the Duala form the core. Its name covers both an ancient political and religious institution and the annual festival that is today its most visible expression. What all versions of the story have in common, whether they come from historians or from Sawa oral tradition itself, is the unbreakable bond between this people and the Wouri river: the Sawa define themselves as the “people of the water”, and the Ngondo is the way they have kept this bond alive, season after season, for generations.

At the heart of Sawa belief are the jengu (plural miengu), water spirits regarded as intermediaries between the living, the ancestors and the divine. Tradition holds that the Duala ancestors rest in the depths of the river and the sea, and that the miengu watch over their descendants while being able to bring them luck or warnings. The most sacred moment of the Ngondo is precisely the encounter with this invisible world: on the banks of the Wouri, on the first Sunday of December, a traditional priest or an initiate dives from a sacred canoe with a vessel holding the wishes and concerns of the community, stays underwater for a time that oral tradition describes as extraordinarily long, then brings the vessel back up. The message drawn from it is believed to guide the collective life of the Sawa until the next assembly. This dimension of the ritual, handed down through initiation and not through detailed public accounts, remains largely a matter of oral tradition: no independent academic source documents precisely how it unfolds, which is consistent with its deliberately reserved, initiates-only nature.

Around this sacred core, the Ngondo grew over the 20th century to include a festive, popular side that is much better documented: a caravan travels through the traditional Sawa districts with artistic shows, a traditional wrestling tournament, a crafts and trade fair, a beauty contest (Miss Ngondo), and the most spectacular event for the general public, the giant canoe race on the Wouri, which can bring together boats with several dozen rowers. The recent history of the Ngondo is not linear, however: the sources agree that the festival went through at least one interruption imposed by the Cameroonian authorities before being restored, but they differ on the exact dates and the precise nature of this interruption. Some mention a celebration linked to 12 July, the anniversary of a colonial-era treaty, interrupted after 1972 and then revived in a form moved to December; others mention a ban in 1981 and a restoration in 1991. These two accounts are not necessarily incompatible (they could describe two different moments in the history of the Ngondo), but no source consulted clearly reconciles them: this point therefore remains debated.

What is firmly established, on the other hand, is the recent international recognition of the tradition. In December 2024, in Asunción (Paraguay), UNESCO's Intergovernmental Committee for the Safeguarding of the Intangible Cultural Heritage inscribed the Ngondo, officially described as “the cult of water oracles and associated cultural traditions among the Sawa”, on the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. This inscription followed that of the Nguon, the great Bamum assembly, a year earlier: two Cameroonian royal and river traditions recognised one after the other by the international institution, a source of pride widely echoed by the Sawa traditional authorities and the Cameroonian government.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Ngondo is the great traditional assembly of the Sawa people, centred on the cult of the water spirits (jengu/miengu) and practised in Douala on the banks of the Wouri"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "Sawa belief places the Duala ancestors in the depths of the river and the sea, watched over by the jengu spirits"}, {"statut": "documented (for the existence of the ritual, as described by UNESCO) / oral tradition (for how it precisely unfolds, reserved for initiates)", "affirmation": "The central ritual consists of an initiate immersing a sacred vessel and coming back up with a message meant to guide the community"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The festival includes a festive side: a caravan, traditional wrestling, a crafts fair, a beauty contest and a canoe race, running from September to the first Sunday of December"}, {"statut": "documented (the fact of the interruption)", "affirmation": "The Ngondo went through an interruption imposed by the Cameroonian authorities before being restored"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The precise dates of this interruption (linked to 12 July and 1972, or a ban from 1981 to 1991) vary depending on the sources and are not reconciled"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "UNESCO inscribed the Ngondo on the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in December 2024"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "This inscription followed that of the Nguon (Bamum people) in December 2023, the first Cameroonian inscription on this list"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$le-ngondo$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$Among the Sawa, the canoe has never been a mere means of transport. In the Duala language, it is called “bɔlɔ”, which oral tradition derives from “bɔ” (end) and “lɔ” (drowning), literally “the end of drownings”: a name that says everything about the place of this boat in the life of a people that has always defined itself by its relationship with the river and the ocean. Its making follows a set of rules in which craft skill is closely intertwined with the sacred: the wood is chosen and worked according to rites handed down from generation to generation, and the launch of a new canoe is accompanied, according to Sawa oral tradition, by offerings meant to entrust it to the water deities who, in Sawa cosmology, govern the river.

It was on the largest of these canoes, the “myolo”, boats that could reach 20 to 28 metres in length and bring together several dozen rowers, that the tangué was fixed: a removable prow piece, carved from a light, hard-wearing wood (bokuka wood is mentioned for at least one documented example), featuring hybrid figures, stylised animals (felines, snakes, sea birds such as the cormorant), sometimes a half-human, half-ape figure holding a harpoon, the whole enhanced with bright colours in which red dominated. This tangué was not mere decoration: its function was both magico-religious, to protect the crew and ensure its victory in the traditional customary races (the “pembisan”), and social, to publicly display the economic power and prestige of the Duala clan that had commissioned it. The craftsmen who carved them remained, for the most part, anonymous: the piece itself, and the clan it represented, took precedence over any individual signature.

These canoes and their carved prows were also direct witnesses of the often violent encounter between the Sawa world and the European powers. In the 19th century, they carried the river and sea trade that made the Duala chieftaincies rich. Under German colonisation, they were also exploited in rituals of power: the tangué of the Lock Priso Bell clan, for example, was carried off by the German doctor Max Buchner during a punitive expedition in 1884, and is now in the State Museum of Ethnology in Munich, a piece whose return has been claimed by the descendant Alexandre Kum'a Ndumbe III. Other tangués joined Western private and museum collections over the course of the 20th century: one of the best-documented examples, acquired around 1957 by the researcher Pierre Harter from Prince Ekwala Essaka of Deido, joined the Jean-Paul Chazal collection and was shown at the Kunsthaus Zürich in 1970-1971, still keeping its original red colouring, the mark, specialists say, of its actual use in races.

Today, the canoe race remains the most spectacular moment of the Ngondo, the great Sawa assembly celebrated every year on the Wouri. Although the oldest tangués have largely left the riverbanks for museums and collections, the object remains a strong symbol of Sawa identity: proof that a people of fishermen and traders knew how to turn a simple sailing ornament into a true work of political and spiritual art.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The Duala canoe is called “bɔlɔ”, which oral tradition translates as “end of drownings” (bɔ = end, lɔ = drowning)"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The making of a canoe and its launch are accompanied by rites meant to entrust it to the water deities"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The great Duala racing canoes, known as “myolo”, can be 20 to 28 metres long"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The tangué is a removable wooden prow, carved and painted, fixed to the front of myolo canoes"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The tangué had both a magico-religious function (protection, victory in the race) and a social one (displaying the clan's prestige and economic power)"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The names of the craftsmen who carved tangués generally remain unknown"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The tangué of the Lock Priso Bell clan was carried off by the German doctor Max Buchner in 1884 and is now in the State Museum of Ethnology in Munich"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "A descendant of the Bell family, Alexandre Kum'a Ndumbe III, has requested the return of this tangué"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "A documented tangué (bokuka wood, 73 x 120 x 44 cm) was acquired around 1957 by Pierre Harter from Prince Ekwala Essaka of Deido, before joining the Jean-Paul Chazal collection"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The “pembisan”/myolo canoe race is one of the oldest customary ceremonies still practised in Douala, documented as early as 1960 by Pierre Harter"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$le-tangue-proue-de-pirogue-sacree$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$The Nguon is one of the most remarkable governance rituals in Central Africa: for three days, every two years (in even years, usually in early December), the Bamum kingdom gathers around a simple and demanding question: how has the king, the Mfon, exercised his power since the last edition? Bamum oral tradition links the origin of this practice to the very founding of the kingdom, at the end of the 14th century, and originally ties it to the harvests and to the beetle, an insect whose swarming flight is said to have given the Bamum ancestors an image of collective cohesion, the very cohesion the ritual seeks to maintain.

The Nguon unfolds in three main phases. First comes the Shâpam, a phase of ritual preparation in which the king receives from the customary chiefs elements believed to renew his strength and wisdom. Then comes the most spectacular moment, the Kûmutngu: the ritual chiefs, having discreetly gathered the people's grievances beforehand, publicly set out before the king the failings of his reign, a kind of popular trial at the end of which he can be sanctioned, or even, in the most serious cases according to tradition, removed. If he obtains a renewed mandate, the king gives a speech of acceptance and forgiveness is sealed. The third phase, the Shômelue, takes the form of a warrior re-enactment replaying episodes from the kingdom's past, followed by a triumphal parade that closes the ceremony amid collective jubilation.

This tradition did not come through the 20th century unbroken. The French colonial authorities, probably judging the public questioning of the sovereign incompatible with the colonial order, banned its celebration from 1924, during the reign of King Ibrahim Njoya. The Nguon came back only very gradually in the following decades, with occasional celebrations, before being fully restored as a great regular event of the kingdom in the early 1990s, under the reign of King Ibrahim Mbombo Njoya, and celebrated every two years since then. In December 2023, UNESCO inscribed the Nguon on its Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, recognising both its cultural value and its universal reach: a ritual mechanism of dialogue, accountability and reconciliation between a sovereign and his people.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Nguon is a ritual practice more than six centuries old among the Bamum"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "Oral tradition links the origin of the Nguon to the founding of the kingdom by Nchare Yen, at the end of the 14th century"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The name “Nguon” is said to have originally referred to a beetle whose flight inspired values of cohesion in the Bamum ancestors"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Nguon is held every two years (even years), usually in early December, over a period of three days"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The ritual unfolds in three main phases: Shâpam (ritual preparation of the king), Kûmutngu (public judgement of his rule), Shômelue (warrior re-enactment and triumphal parade)"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The judged king can be sanctioned, or even removed in the event of serious failings according to tradition"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Nguon was banned by the French colonial administration from 1924, during the reign of King Ibrahim Njoya"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Nguon was gradually revived from the late 1950s, then restored as a great regular biennial event in the early 1990s"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Nguon was inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in December 2023"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$nguon$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$Describing a “Beti/Bulu traditional chief” the way one would describe a Bamum fon would be misleading, and that is precisely what makes this figure interesting. The ethnologists who have studied Beti, Bulu and Fang societies in depth, foremost among them Philippe Laburthe-Tolra, agree that these peoples did not have, before the colonial period, a centralised political organisation comparable to the kingdoms of western Cameroon: no dynasty, no single capital, no sovereign whose authority extended beyond a village or a group of related villages. Some researchers describe this organisation as “acephalous”, literally “headless”; others qualify this term, seen as somewhat reductive, by pointing out that the absence of centralised political power did not prevent the existence of differentiated social statuses and a very real hierarchy at the local level. The debate over the exact term remains open among specialists; what there is consensus on, however, is the absence of a centralised power of a royal kind.

In this setting, the figure closest to a “man of authority” is the nkukuma, literally the “truly rich man”. But wealth alone was not enough: oral tradition and the work of ethnologists report that a notable worthy of the name had to combine several qualities at once: valour and courage (ayog), the gift of speech and mastery of proverbs to settle conflicts fairly, and above all generosity (akàb), regarded as the cardinal virtue: a nkukuma's wealth only made sense if it was shared and redistributed, first to his descendants and relatives, then more widely to those who placed themselves under his protection. Seniority (the principle of ntol, precedence linked to age) also counted, but remained secondary to real ability: a younger but more capable man could surpass a less gifted elder in influence. A particularly recognised nkukuma could attain the title of njoé bôt, a form of chief, but whose authority remained limited to his village or group of villages, without binding power over subjects in the sense a centralised monarchy would understand it. It was notably within initiation societies such as the Sô that these established men found a place to exercise their influence, by joining the circle of initiates consulted on community affairs.

Finally, it should be noted that the more formalised figure of the “chief” that colonial history often associates with the Beti and the Bulu (canton chief, paramount chief) is to a large extent a German and then French administrative creation, which appointed or made official certain local notables to serve as relays for colonial authority, a practice well documented by historians of colonial Cameroon but which must not be confused with the precolonial political organisation itself.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Precolonial Beti/Bulu societies had no centralised political organisation comparable to a monarchy (no single dynasty, no royal capital)"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The term “acephalous society” used to describe this organisation is debated among specialists: some see it as reductive because it does not rule out local social hierarchies"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The nkukuma refers to a notable whose authority rests on redistributed wealth, eloquence, courage and generosity, more than on heredity alone"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "A recognised nkukuma could attain the title of njoé bôt, a form of chief whose authority was limited to a village or a group of villages"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The principle of precedence by age (ntol) existed but could be overtaken by individual ability"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The “canton chiefs” or “paramount chiefs” associated with the Beti/Bulu during the colonial period are to a large extent creations or official appointments of the German and then French administration, distinct from the traditional authority of the nkukuma"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$nkukuma-notable-lignage$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$Foumban has been the capital of the Bamum kingdom since its founding, at the end of the 14th century, by Prince Nchare Yen, who came from the Tikar dynasty. For generations, successive sovereigns had their residence built there, rebuilt several times over the reigns and the twists of history: the earlier palaces, made of bamboo and thatch, were destroyed by fires before King Ibrahim Njoya, the best known of the Bamum sovereigns, had the stone and brick structure that still stands in Foumban today built at the very beginning of the 20th century. The result is an architecture unique in Central Africa, halfway between a princely residence and the look of a fortress, which local popular tradition links to Njoya's admiration for the German governor's residence he had visited in Buea.

The palace is not a frozen monument: it has remained, to this day, the actual residence of the Bamum sovereign, while housing within it a museum that presents part of the royal heritage to the public, from headdresses and regalia to musical instruments and manuscripts linked to the Bamum script. In the 1990s, a renovation programme supported by UNESCO made it possible to restore the building, which was at risk of deteriorating, and to give it back some of its original splendour.

More recently, in April 2024, a large new Bamum Royal Museum (about 5,000 m²) was inaugurated a few steps from the historic palace. It now brings together most of the royal collections, several thousand objects accumulated over six centuries of successive reigns, offering a larger and better-equipped setting to preserve this heritage, while the palace lives on as a place of residence and ceremonies. This gradual dispersal of the royal collections into museums, Cameroonian but also European, regularly revives the debate on the return of objects taken during the colonial period, a subject still open and discussed both by historians and by the Bamum authorities themselves.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Bamum kingdom was founded at the end of the 14th century by Prince Nchare Yen, from the Tikar dynasty"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Foumban has been the historic capital of the kingdom since its founding"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The current palace was rebuilt at the very beginning of the 20th century under the reign of King Ibrahim Njoya, after the earlier bamboo and thatch palaces were destroyed by fire"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The precise completion date of the current palace varies depending on the sources (around 1917-1921)"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "Njoya is said to have drawn inspiration from the German governor's residence in Buea to design the plans of the palace"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The palace was the subject of a renovation programme supported by UNESCO in the 1990s"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "A new Bamum Royal Museum, next to the palace, was inaugurated on 13 April 2024 and brings together several thousand objects covering six centuries of history"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The palace still houses the residence of the reigning Bamum sovereign today"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The return of Bamum heritage objects taken during the colonial period remains a subject of current debate"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$palais-royal-foumban$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$Among the Beti and Bulu peoples and, more broadly, across the Pahouin group of Central Africa, the Sô held a central place among the institutions that organised the passage to adulthood. It was not just an individual rite of passage: according to the work of the anthropologist Philippe Laburthe-Tolra, who devoted part of his major work to Beti religion, it combined two closely intertwined functions. On the one hand, a dimension of collective atonement (the nsem), in which a transgression publicly acknowledged by a member of the community triggered the organisation of the rite. On the other, a genuine initiation journey for a group of young boys, generally aged around twelve to fifteen, who came out of it recognised as accomplished men.

The process stretched over several months and followed a series of ritual stages: public confession of the fault at the origin of the rite, selection of the candidates (each guided by a sponsor), planting of a sacred horn near a symbolic tree, then the felling of this tree, a series of physical and symbolic ordeals lived in the forest, construction of a dedicated sanctuary, and finally a period of seclusion devoted to the teaching of the young initiates by the elders. A notable organisational detail, noted by several sources, is that the main officiant of the rite always had to be an outsider to the lineages directly concerned, a way of guaranteeing a form of impartiality in a ritual that dealt with matters of fault and reparation within the community. At the end of the journey, the young man gained rights and marks of status reserved for initiates, notably the right to eat certain foods until then forbidden to women and the uninitiated, and joined the circle of men consulted on village affairs.

The precise detail of the ordeals undergone in the forest remains, by its very nature, knowledge reserved for initiates and handed down orally from generation to generation; it is not for a popular-education platform to detail its content, and respecting this secret is in fact an integral part of what the rite sought to pass on. What is well documented, on the other hand, is that the Sô was deeply shaken by the encounter between Beti-Fang societies and Christianity during the colonial period: missionaries and the colonial administration, depending on the region and the period, sought to ban it, compete with it or replace it with other forms of guidance for young people. The rite, in its old and complete form, thus largely disappeared during the 20th century; today it survives mainly as an object of memory, academic research and, occasionally, attempts at cultural revival, rather than as a widespread living practice.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Sô was a male initiation rite practised among the Beti, the Bulu and other Pahouin groups of Central Africa"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "It combined a function of collective atonement (nsem) and a function of passage to adulthood for a group of boys"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The candidates were generally between about 12 and 15 years old"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The main officiant of the rite (zomoloa) had to be an outsider to the lineages directly concerned by the fault at the origin of the rite"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The detailed course of the rite (stages, symbols, exact duration) is documented by academic anthropology but varies depending on the sub-groups and the periods described"}, {"statut": "tradition_orale", "affirmation": "The precise content of the ordeals lived in seclusion belongs to protected initiation knowledge, not detailed publicly as an editorial choice"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The rite, in its old and complete form, largely disappeared during the colonial and post-colonial period under the effect of evangelisation and the colonial administration"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$rite-initiatique-so$t$;

update public.decouverte_items set
  contenu_en_texte = $t$In Bamum royal art, no object captures the sovereign's prestige better than his throne. The Mandu Yenu, whose name means “rich in beads” in the Bamum language, is the most accomplished example: a piece in two parts, a seat and a footrest, carved from solid wood and then entirely covered with a layer of glass beads imported from Europe and cowries from the Indian Ocean, sewn one by one onto the wooden frame in precise patterns. The carvings that structure the whole are not decorative in the Western sense: they carry the symbols of Bamum power, starting with the two-headed snake and the earth spider, a figure associated with wisdom and divination, as well as human figures representing royal protection and the military strength of the kingdom. To bring out this throne in ceremonies was therefore literally to sit on the symbols of the legitimacy of power.

The history of this particular throne changed course in 1908, when King Ibrahim Njoya gave it to the German emperor Wilhelm II, at a time when the Bamum kingdom was under German colonial administration. Accounts of the exact circumstances of this gift differ: some sources link it to a military expedition carried out jointly by German colonial troops and those of the Bamum kingdom against the neighbouring kingdom of Nso', others mention a request from the German authorities, who initially wanted a replica of the throne for the emperor's birthday before obtaining the original object. What is certain is that Njoya kept a replica of the throne in Foumban while the original headed for Berlin. This story raises a question today to which historians and museum institutions give no single answer: can a gift made in the context of deeply unequal colonial power relations truly be called a free gift? It is this tension, between artistic masterpiece and object of colonial memory, that continues to shape the way the Mandu Yenu is seen.

Today, the original throne is held at the Ethnologisches Museum in Berlin, where it remains one of the most documented pieces of Bamum art outside the African continent, while the royal palace of Foumban continues to house its replica and other beaded thrones, living witnesses of a tradition of textile and bead craftsmanship still practised today by Bamum artisans.$t$,
  statut_fait_legende_en = $t$[{"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The Mandu Yenu is a Bamum royal throne made up of a seat and a footrest carved from wood"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "It is entirely covered with glass beads imported from Europe and cowries from the Indian Ocean"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The carvings represent royal symbols, notably the two-headed snake and the earth spider associated with wisdom"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "King Ibrahim Njoya gave the throne to the German emperor Wilhelm II in 1908"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "The precise circumstances of this gift (link to a joint military expedition against the Nso', or an initial request for a replica for the emperor's birthday) vary depending on the sources"}, {"statut": "debattu", "affirmation": "Whether this gift can be called fully voluntary, given the colonial context, is debated among historians and museum institutions"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "Njoya is said to have had a replica of the throne made, kept at the Foumban palace"}, {"statut": "atteste", "affirmation": "The original throne is now held at the Ethnologisches Museum in Berlin"}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$trone-mandu-yenu$t$;

update public.mythes set
  recit_chapitres_en = $t$[{"numero": 1, "titre": "Origins: the world beneath the waters", "texte": "Listen to the sound of the ocean as it dies on the Cameroonian coast, where the Wouri river opens into the Atlantic. Listen closely, because the Sawa, who have lived on this shore for generations, will tell you that this water is never empty. Beneath the surface, in the rivers as in the sea, a whole people breathes, a people seen only on rare occasions, and only by those worthy of seeing them. They are called the miengu. In the singular, a jengu. Among the Bakweri, further south, they say liengu, and maengu in the plural. The names change from one Sawa people to another, but the belief runs along the whole coast.\n\nThe elders say they look like beings halfway between human and mermaid, their skin often very light, their hair long, their mouths marked by a fine gap between the teeth, which the Sawa have always considered a sign of beauty. They live in the depths, in a kingdom beneath the waters of the Wouri and the ocean, a kingdom said to be rich and orderly, like the world of the living but reversed, slower, quieter."}, {"numero": 2, "titre": "Powers and the choice", "texte": "But do not think the miengu are content to live hidden away. They watch what happens above them, and they choose. They choose who, among men and women, deserves their favour. They are said to bring four blessings to those they honour: plenty of shrimp and fish in the nets, victory in the great canoe races that are the pride of the Sawa villages, protection against sickness and epidemics, and an end to the overly long rains that drown the harvests. A jengu can also choose a human being, often a young girl, to be its go-between with the visible world: she becomes a healer, initiated into a secret society led by a master of ceremonies called the ekale, the one who once wore a mask to speak on behalf of the water spirits. Among the Bakweri, this passage through the world of the liengu is a full part of the rite that leads a young girl into adulthood."}, {"numero": 3, "titre": "The clash: resistance and the mystery of the diver", "texte": "It must also be said, plainly, that the world of the miengu has not always been welcomed with open arms. When European missionaries arrived on this coast, they saw this cult as something to fight, a pagan belief to stamp out. The Sawa never stopped believing, and the cult went on living, discreetly, throughout the nineteenth century, documented by administrators and later by the researchers who came to study these secret societies.\n\nAnd every year, in Douala, this ancient belief rises to the surface, quite literally, during the great Ngondo festival. Since this festival was revived in 1949 as a moment of gathering and pride for the Sawa people, its high point has been a ritual that sends a shiver through everyone who witnesses it: a diver, chosen and prepared, plunges into the waters of the Wouri with a calabash, and stays there, witnesses say, for between three and ten minutes. Time stretches, the crowd holds its breath, and when he finally comes back up, it is said he returns from the kingdom of the miengu, carrying a message, a prophecy for the year that is beginning. No one knows exactly what happens under the water during those minutes. That is precisely the heart of the Sawa mystery: a belief that can be neither proven nor disproven, yet which, year after year, keeps bringing a whole people together around its river."}, {"numero": 4, "titre": "Legacy: a living presence today", "texte": "This is what the Sawa have said for generations, and what they still say today, without time having worn down their conviction. The miengu are not a bedtime story for children. For many on this coast, they are a real presence, respected, honoured before going out to sea, called upon for health, celebrated every year before a whole people gathered together. If you come from this land where the water itself is inhabited, remember that your ancestors never saw nature as an empty backdrop. They saw a living, peopled world, one they had to come to terms with, respectfully. Stand tall before the ocean. You come from there."}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$mythe-miengu-esprits-eau-sawa$t$;

update public.mythes set
  recit_chapitres_en = $t$[{"numero": 1, "titre": "Rifum, three heirs for a single throne", "texte": "Listen. Towards the end of the fourteenth century, in the highlands of western Cameroon, a people of Tikar origin still lives united under the authority of a single chief, at Rifum, not far from present-day Mbankim. But kingdoms, like families, do not always stay united. Three children of this chief grow up, each with the idea that power might one day fall to them: two brothers, Nchare and Koumjouom, and their sister Mfom.\n\nKoumjouom is one of those people everyone sees as a king from the start. Tall, built for strength, sure of himself to the point of arrogance. Nchare, for his part, is small, slender, almost frail next to his brother. And yet, in the people's hearts, it is towards Nchare that eyes turn, for where Koumjouom scorns, Nchare listens. Where one imposes, the other shares. Modesty, in that world as in many others, sometimes weighs more than stature."}, {"numero": 2, "titre": "The trial of the race is prepared", "texte": "And so, Bamum tradition says, it was decided to settle the question of power through a simple trial, almost childlike in its principle: a race. The three contenders sit on the same tree trunk, about a hundred metres from a stone set in the distance. The first to set foot on that stone becomes king. Koumjouom, athletic, is already celebrating in advance. He knows the race is won before the starting signal is even given."}, {"numero": 3, "titre": "The rigged race", "texte": "But in the shadow of this contest, someone is watching over Nchare. A counsellor named Njimongha discreetly approaches Mfom before the start and whispers a few words to her. The young woman goes to sit on the trunk, as planned, but as she settles, she traps a fold of her brother Koumjouom's loincloth under her weight, without him noticing. When the starting signal comes, Koumjouom leaps up and finds himself held back, slowed down, as if an invisible obstacle tied him to the ground for a few decisive seconds. Those seconds are enough. Nchare dashes off, covers the distance, and is the first to set foot on the stone. The cries of victory drown out Koumjouom's protests, too late to change the outcome of the race. Nchare Yen is proclaimed king."}, {"numero": 4, "titre": "The birth of the Bamum kingdom", "texte": "From this victory a kingdom is born. Nchare Yen, at the head of two to three hundred followers, crosses the Mapé river and settles in the region where Foumban stands today. He subdues seven local principalities, including the Pangié people who then occupied the territory of present-day Magba, and sets his first capital at Njimom. A founding pact, handed down ever since by Bamum tradition, seals this birth in a simple formula: the Bamum State is born, and Nchare is its king, with succession assured among his sons. Seven kom counsellors, the very ones who had enthroned him, become the hereditary guardians of this founding law.\n\nNchare Yen thus reigned, according to tradition, from 1394 to 1418, the first of a line of Bamum sovereigns, the mfon, that would continue for nearly five centuries, until the kingdom lost its independence at the end of the nineteenth century. A late descendant of this line, much later, would become one of the most famous sovereigns of Central Africa: King Njoya, inventor of a script of his people's own, already told elsewhere in this collection. But it all begins here, with this small man with a round belly, as people said, brave despite his puny look, fond of dancing and palm wine, whose cunning, or that of his supporters, was worth more than his brother's brute strength.\n\nIt must be said honestly: what we know of Nchare Yen comes almost entirely from Bamum oral tradition, handed down from generation to generation, then written down much later by the kingdom's chroniclers and the historians who studied them. No witness from 1394 left any writing dated from that time. It is a founding story, in the full sense of the word: it founds an identity, it explains a legitimacy, it is not an official record.\n\nRemember this: kingdoms are not always born of the force of arms alone. This one was born of a trick, of a sister who chose her side, of a small man preferred by his people to a proud giant. If you come from this land of western Cameroon, remember that greatness is never measured by build. Stand tall. You come from there."}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$mythe-nchare-yen-bamoun$t$;

update public.mythes set
  recit_chapitres_en = $t$[{"numero": 1, "titre": "Nanga and the children of the savannah (Origins)", "texte": "Listen. There was a time when the people we now call the Beti did not live in the deep forest of Central Cameroon, but much further north, towards the Adamawa. Tradition has them all descend from a single ancestor, Nanga, a Bantu man described as albino, and from his seven children: Kolo Beti, Eton Beti, Mvele Beti, Mvan Beti, Meka Beti, Bulu and Ntumu. From these seven, over the generations, would come the great branches that still recognise one another as cousins today: the Eton, the Ewondo, the Bene, the Bulu, the Manguissa, the Ntumu, and beyond today's borders, the Fang of Gabon and Equatorial Guinea."}, {"numero": 2, "titre": "Pressure from the north and the flight", "texte": "But this people did not stay forever in its northern lands. The pressure was growing, the stories say, coming from Muslim Fulani peoples determined to impose their faith on all the animist peoples of the region. Many popular traditions link this pressure to the name of Usman dan Fodio, a Fulani leader and figure of the great jihad of the early nineteenth century. It must be said honestly, here as everywhere I open my mouth for you: historians agree less easily on this precise point than the legend would have you believe. Dan Fodio's movement began at the very start of the 1800s, whereas the Beti migration southwards probably stretched over a longer period, across several generations, between the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Keep the right idea, the pressure of raids and Islamisation coming from the north, but stay cautious about the precise name attached to it."}, {"numero": 3, "titre": "The living bridge over the Sanaga", "texte": "So, fleeing south, through dense and hostile forest, exhausted by days of walking, the Beti finally come up against an obstacle that nothing seemed able to cross: the Sanaga river, wide, powerful, impassable for a whole people burdened with its children and its belongings. It was there, the elders say, that God, in his love for this fleeing people, sent them help in a form no one expected: a gigantic, mystical serpent named Ngan Medza. The creature stretched itself from one bank of the river to the other, offering its own back as a living bridge, and the people, family after family, began the crossing.\n\nNight was already falling. The one carrying the bamboo torch to light the way was at the back of the group, and as the line moved forward, the flame grew weaker. The stories differ on what happened next, and I will not hide this disagreement from you rather than decide for you: some say that a warrior, in the darkness, drove his spear into the serpent's back, mistaking it for a stranded log or giving in to a fatal curiosity; others say it was the very flame of the torch that fell on Ngan Medza's spine. In both versions, the result is the same: wounded, the serpent reared up, tipping into the water those who had not yet finished crossing. Those who could take refuge in the forest on the other bank stayed there. That, tradition says, is how the Beti people ended up scattered on both sides of the Sanaga river, as they have remained to this day."}, {"numero": 4, "titre": "The memory of the serpent (Legacy)", "texte": "That serpent has never been forgotten. It is said that before disappearing into the water, Ngan Medza gave its people advice on living in harmony with nature and warned them of the dangers to come. Since then, Beti sculptors carve its image in wood, traditional songs still carry its name, and some communities hold a festival in its memory every year, Ngan Medza remaining, far more than a mere animal of myth, the living symbol of a people's passage from an old world to the land it still lives on.\n\nIf you are Ewondo, Eton, Bulu, Bene or Fang, or if you simply carry within you this same memory of forest and river, remember this: your people crossed the uncrossable. It was carried, one day, by something greater than itself, and it paid for that crossing with a loss, as every passage to a new land always costs one. Stand tall before the Sanaga. You come from there."}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$mythe-ngan-medza-beti$t$;

update public.mythes set
  recit_chapitres_en = $t$[{"numero": 1, "titre": "The rock and the hunt", "texte": "Listen. Eighty kilometres from Édéa, where the Cameroonian forest closes in on itself and the Sanaga river makes its way towards the ocean, there is a mountain that is not quite a mountain like the others. It is called Ngog Lituba, which means, in the Bassa language, the pierced rock. A rock rising from the forest to fifteen hundred metres, with, on its side, an opening, a cave, a mouth of stone at eight hundred metres of altitude. And it was from this very mouth, the Bassa, the Bakoko and the Bati have said for generations, that their ancestors came out into the world."}, {"numero": 2, "titre": "The web that saves a people", "texte": "Here is what tradition tells. A very long time ago, two ancestors were fleeing a persecution that came from very far away, from an ancient land that some stories name as ancient Egypt. Pursued, hunted, they find refuge in the belly of this rock, in this cave hidden in the heart of the forest. And there, a tiny miracle saves them: a spider, in a single night, weaves its web across the entrance. When the pursuers arrive before Ngog Lituba, they see this web intact, untouched, and they tell themselves that no one can have passed through this opening for a long time, that there is nothing to find there. They leave. The thread of a simple spider was enough to save a whole people from extermination."}, {"numero": 3, "titre": "The children of the pierced rock", "texte": "It was from this refuge, the elders say, that the children of the pierced rock were born, those the Bassa still call Bon ba Ngok Lituba today. From generation to generation, the population grew in and around this cave, until the land itself could no longer hold it. Then, in successive waves, the clans scattered from Ngog Lituba, spread across the forest of southern Cameroon, and gave birth to the different groups that recognise one another as cousins today: the Bassa, the Bakoko, also called Elog Mpo'o, and the Bati. Some traditions go further still and see in Ngog Lituba not only the cradle of these three peoples, but a resting place for the ancestors of all Africa, the first source of a wider federation that the old stories call the Nnanga federation.\n\nScience looks at this mountain differently, neither contradicting nor confirming the myth. The geologists who have observed the rock note that it bears the marks of volcanic rock, swollen in places as if it had once known extreme heat, and some put forward the hypothesis of an ancient crater, volcanic or even meteoritic, worn down by thousands of years of rain and wind. It is also said that very old footprints can still be read in the stone, as if the rock itself kept the memory of those who once lived in it, between the tenth and thirteenth centuries, before these populations migrated to the banks of the Wouri river, further north."}, {"numero": 4, "titre": "A living sacred place", "texte": "Ngog Lituba is not a dead memory. It is a living sacred place, a place of memory that the Bassa guard jealously. In 1959, when a bishop had a large cross and a statue of the Virgin erected on the summit to make it a Catholic pilgrimage site, the communities who consider this mountain sacred felt dispossessed, to the point of carrying this wound all the way to the United Nations bodies, in Geneva and New York, in 2006, to ask that the sacred character of the site be recognised for its peoples of origin. That shows how much this story does not belong to the past: it continues to shape the identity and dignity of a whole people today.\n\nIf you are Bassa, Bakoko or Bati, or simply if you want to understand how a people tells its own story, remember this: before being a nation, before being a language, before being a territory, a people is first of all a story it passes on about its own origins. And the story of the children of the pierced rock says something simple and powerful: that even hunted, even cornered at the back of a cave, a people can find refuge, survive, and one day spread across a whole forest. Stand tall. You come from there."}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$mythe-ngog-lituba-bassa$t$;

update public.mythes set
  recit_chapitres_en = $t$[{"numero": 1, "titre": "The legend of the giants", "texte": "Listen. In the far north of Cameroon, where the land flattens out all the way to the shores of Lake Chad, a people still tells today the story of those who came before it on this land: the Sao. And what the Kotoko tell of their ancestors is no ordinary story, for the Sao, they say, were not men like any others. They were giants.\n\nGiants of a strength the imagination struggles to hold. It is said they used whole trunks of borassus palms as mere walking sticks, when you and I already struggle to carry a branch. It is said that one of them alone could swallow, in a single meal, the load of millet a camel takes a whole day to carry. And it is said, more astonishing still, that a Sao chief from the village of Goulfey only had to sneeze for the birds, for a league around, to take flight all at once, frightened by the breath of a single man. That is the measure the traditions of Lake Chad give to this ancient people: men of superhuman power, who came, several stories say, from very far to the east, some tracing them back to the Nile valley, to the Egypt of the pharaohs itself."}, {"numero": 2, "titre": "The earth speaks: the real Sao civilisation", "texte": "So much for the legend. And now, listen to the second half of this story, the one the earth itself ended up revealing.\n\nFor the Sao, in reality, did not vanish into the silence of oral stories alone. A French archaeologist, Jean-Paul Lebeuf, accompanied by his wife, spent a large part of his life, from the middle of the twentieth century, excavating the shores of Lake Chad and the Logone river, in this region straddling today's Cameroon, Chad and Nigeria. And what he found was no longer a myth: thousands of pottery shards, bronze sculptures, towns surrounded by earthen walls, necropolises where the dead lay in great funerary jars. According to the dates established by the Lebeufs, Sao culture is said to have appeared around Lake Chad as early as the sixth century before our era, reaching its peak several centuries later, before its towns were destroyed in the sixteenth century of our era. It is not legendary giants that archaeology unearthed, but craftsmen, builders, potters of immense talent, whose civilisation spread over a considerable territory around the lake."}, {"numero": 3, "titre": "From the fall of the towns to the heir", "texte": "And it was from these builders, south of Lake Chad, that a new people rose after their fall, from the sixteenth century onwards: the Kotoko. The Kotoko do not tell their history as a break with the Sao, but as a continuity, a direct descent. They call themselves their heirs, their descendants, and the very organisation of their sultanates, at Kousséri, Mara, Makari, Goulfey and Logone Birni, still bears its mark. Among the Kotoko, one does not become a prince by birth alone: the claimant chosen by the dignitaries must go through long ritual ordeals before being enthroned by the guardian animals and the representatives of the town's oldest families, to be recognised as the direct heir of the Sao sovereigns. The myth of the giants has therefore not remained locked in the past: even today, it continues to legitimise and structure power in these lakeside towns."}, {"numero": 4, "titre": "Two faces, one truth", "texte": "So here is a story with two faces, and I tell it to you with both, because that is the whole truth: on one side, the legend of giants able to swallow a camel's load and send the birds fleeing with a sneeze, who perhaps came from the banks of the Nile; on the other, a very real civilisation, dated, excavated, measured by science, which built towns on the shores of Lake Chad centuries before Europe ever laid eyes on this region. The two answer each other, feed each other, without ever merging.\n\nIf you come from this land of Cameroon's Far North, remember that your history did not need to be invented to be great. A whole, real civilisation came before yours on this land, and the memory it left was so immense that, to tell it, people had to speak of giants. Stand tall. You come from there."}]$t$::jsonb
where slug = $t$mythe-sao-geants-kotoko$t$;


-- Contrôle : doit renvoyer heros 7, decouverte 10, mythes 5.
select 'heros' t, count(*) from public.heros where frise_chronologique_en is not null
union all select 'decouverte', count(*) from public.decouverte_items where contenu_en_texte is not null
union all select 'mythes', count(*) from public.mythes where jsonb_array_length(recit_chapitres_en) > 0;
